<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="reed-trigonometry">
  <title>Trigonometry</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>A point traveling around a circle creates a repeating wave when we record
    one of its coordinates. This connects the geometry of the unit circle to the
    family of functions</p>
    <p><md>f(x)=A\cos(wx+T)+C.</md></p>
    <p>By the end of this lesson, you will be able to read sine and cosine from
    the unit circle, use sine and cosine to find sides of right triangles,
    explain why <m>\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1</m>, and use
    <m>A</m>, <m>w</m>, <m>T</m>, and <m>C</m> to describe and sketch a
    cosine wave, including its vertical displacement.
    The final page introduces the other four trigonometric functions:
    tangent, cotangent, secant, and cosecant.
    You need the Pythagorean theorem, coordinates, and basic algebra; no previous
    trigonometry lesson is required.</p>
    <p>Work with pencil and paper as you read. The interactive exercises are
    selected from the Math Trailhead's existing trigonometry worksheets.
    Enter exact answers such as <c>pi/3</c> and <c>sqrt(3)/2</c> unless a problem
    requests decimals. Use radians unless a degree symbol appears.</p>
    <p>Each topic below opens on its own page. Start with the unit circle,
    then connect it to right triangles before moving on to identities and
    graphs. Use the Previous, Up, and Next links to move through the lesson.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="reed-trig-circle">
    <title>The unit circle: angles become coordinates</title>
    <p>The <term>unit circle</term> has center <m>(0,0)</m> and radius
    <m>1</m>. Start at <m>(1,0)</m> and turn through an angle <m>\theta</m>.
    Positive angles turn counterclockwise; negative angles turn clockwise.
    The angle determines a point on the circle.</p>
    <p>An angle in <term>radians</term> is the signed distance traveled along a
    circle divided by its radius. On the unit circle, the size of the angle
    equals the arc length. One full turn is <m>2\pi</m> radians because the
    circumference is <m>2\pi</m>. Therefore</p>
    <p><md>360^\circ=2\pi\text{ radians},\qquad
    180^\circ=\pi\text{ radians},\qquad
    90^\circ=\frac{\pi}{2}\text{ radians}.</md></p>
    <p>To convert degrees to radians, multiply by <m>\pi/180</m>; to convert
    radians to degrees, multiply by <m>180/\pi</m>. For example,
    <m>120^\circ=2\pi/3</m> radians and <m>-\pi/4=-45^\circ</m>.</p>
    <p>We define <term>cosine</term> to be the horizontal coordinate and
    <term>sine</term> to be the vertical coordinate:</p>
    <p><md>Q=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta).</md></p>
    <figure xml:id="reed-trig-circle-figure">
      <caption>The radius has length 1. Its horizontal and vertical components
      are cosine and sine.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/unit-circle.svg" width="85%">
        <shortdescription>A unit circle with a radius to a point in the first quadrant.
        The horizontal leg is labeled cosine theta, the vertical leg sine theta,
        and the radius 1. The four axis intercepts are labeled with coordinates
        and angles 0, pi over 2, pi, and 3 pi over 2.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>Dropping a perpendicular from <m>Q</m> to the horizontal axis makes
    a right triangle. Its hypotenuse is the radius, so its length is <m>1</m>.
    On the next page, <xref ref="reed-trig-right-triangles" text="title"/>, we will scale
    this picture to explain sine and cosine for right triangles of any size.
    The coordinate definition also works in every quadrant and on the axes.</p>
    <table>
      <title>Five landmarks in one turn</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes"><cell><m>\theta</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>\pi</m></cell><cell><m>3\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>2\pi</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\cos\theta</m></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\sin\theta</m></cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>Signs come from coordinates: in quadrant I both are positive; in II
    cosine is negative and sine positive; in III both are negative; in IV
    cosine is positive and sine negative. Also, each coordinate lies between
    <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>.</p>
    <p>You can reconstruct the familiar exact values. At <m>\pi/4</m>, the
    triangle's legs have the same length <m>a</m>, so
    <m>2a^2=1</m> gives <m>a=\sqrt{2}/2</m>. Bisect an equilateral triangle
    of side <m>1</m>: the short leg is <m>1/2</m>, the hypotenuse is
    <m>1</m>, and the long leg is <m>\sqrt{3}/2</m>. This gives the
    <m>\pi/6</m> and <m>\pi/3</m> values.</p>
    <table>
      <title>First-quadrant values to reconstruct</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes"><cell>Angle</cell><cell><m>\cos\theta</m></cell><cell><m>\sin\theta</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\pi/6=30^\circ</m></cell><cell><m>\sqrt{3}/2</m></cell><cell><m>1/2</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\pi/4=45^\circ</m></cell><cell><m>\sqrt{2}/2</m></cell><cell><m>\sqrt{2}/2</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>\pi/3=60^\circ</m></cell><cell><m>1/2</m></cell><cell><m>\sqrt{3}/2</m></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>A <term>reference angle</term> is the acute angle from a point's radius
    to the horizontal axis. Reflect a first-quadrant point to obtain other
    quadrants, keeping coordinate magnitudes and changing their signs.
    For example, <m>5\pi/6</m> is in quadrant II with reference angle
    <m>\pi/6</m>, so its point is <m>(-\sqrt{3}/2,1/2)</m>.</p>
    <p>Adding or subtracting <m>2\pi</m> makes one extra full turn and returns
    to the same point. Thus <m>-\pi/6</m> and <m>11\pi/6</m> have the same
    sine and cosine. This is why both functions repeat every <m>2\pi</m>.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-degrees">
      <title>Convert degrees to radians</title>
      <!-- Reused from degrees_radians.ptx. -->
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/AngleMeasure-Radians/convert-degrees2rads-med.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-radians">
      <title>Convert radians to degrees</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/AngleMeasure-Radians/convert-rads2degrees-med.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-quadrants">
      <title>Use coordinate signs to identify quadrants</title>
      <!-- Reused from evaluating_trig_functions.ptx. -->
      <webwork source="Library/Rochester/setTrig02FunctionsUnitCircle/srw5_2_47.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-cosine-values">
      <title>Read exact cosine values</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/UnitCircle/unit-circle-cosine.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-sine-values">
      <title>Read exact sine values</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/UnitCircle/unit-circle-sine.pg"/>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="reed-trig-right-triangles">
    <title>Right triangles and the unit circle</title>
    <p>Choose one acute angle <m>\theta</m> in a right triangle. The
    <term>hypotenuse</term> is the side opposite the right angle; it is always
    the longest side. Of the two remaining sides, the <term>opposite</term>
    side faces <m>\theta</m>, and the <term>adjacent</term> side touches
    <m>\theta</m>. The word adjacent here means the leg, not the hypotenuse.
    Opposite and adjacent depend on which acute angle you choose.</p>
    <figure xml:id="reed-trig-triangle-figure">
      <caption>Divide every side by the hypotenuse <m>r</m>. The angle stays
      the same, and the new hypotenuse is a radius of the unit circle.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/right-triangle.svg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Two similar right triangles with angle theta at the
        lower left. The first has adjacent leg a, opposite leg b, and
        hypotenuse r. The second is scaled by one over r, with hypotenuse 1,
        horizontal leg a over r equal to cosine theta, and vertical leg b over
        r equal to sine theta. Its upper vertex lies on the unit circle.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>Place the chosen angle at the origin and the adjacent side along the
    positive horizontal axis. If the adjacent, opposite, and hypotenuse
    lengths are <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>r</m>, the upper vertex is
    <m>(a,b)</m>. Shrink the triangle by dividing every length by <m>r</m>.
    The new vertex <m>(a/r,b/r)</m> is on the unit circle, at the same angle
    <m>\theta</m>. Its coordinates must therefore be
    <m>(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)</m>. This gives</p>
    <p><md>\boxed{\cos\theta=\frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}}
    =\frac{a}{r}},\qquad
    \boxed{\sin\theta=\frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{hypotenuse}}
    =\frac{b}{r}}.</md></p>
    <p>These ratios depend on the angle, not the size of the triangle.
    Triangles with the same angles are similar: doubling all three side
    lengths leaves both ratios unchanged. This is why one value of
    <m>\sin\theta</m> or <m>\cos\theta</m> works for every right triangle
    with that angle.</p>
    <example xml:id="reed-trig-triangle-ratios">
      <title>A 3–4–5 triangle becomes a point on the circle</title>
      <statement><p>A right triangle has adjacent side <m>4</m>, opposite
      side <m>3</m>, and hypotenuse <m>5</m>, relative to <m>\theta</m>.
      Find its sine and cosine, and describe the corresponding point on the
      unit circle.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>Divide by the hypotenuse:
      <m>\cos\theta=4/5</m> and <m>\sin\theta=3/5</m>. The point is
      <m>(4/5,3/5)</m>. A triangle with sides <m>8,6,10</m> gives exactly
      the same point, since <m>8/10=4/5</m> and <m>6/10=3/5</m>.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Use an angle to find a missing length</title>
      <p>Multiplying the ratios by the hypotenuse gives</p>
      <p><md>\text{adjacent}=r\cos\theta,\qquad
      \text{opposite}=r\sin\theta.</md></p>
      <p>Draw the triangle, mark the chosen angle, and label its sides before
      selecting a formula. If the hypotenuse is unknown, solve the same
      equation for <m>r</m>. For example,
      <m>r=\text{opposite}/\sin\theta</m>.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <example xml:id="reed-trig-ladder">
      <title>A ladder against a wall</title>
      <statement><p>A <m>10</m>-foot ladder makes a <m>60^\circ</m>
      angle with level ground and rests against a vertical wall. How high
      does it reach, and how far is its foot from the wall?</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The ladder is the hypotenuse. Height is opposite the
      angle at the ground; horizontal distance is adjacent. Therefore</p>
      <p><md>\text{height}=10\sin60^\circ=5\sqrt{3}\text{ ft},\qquad
      \text{distance}=10\cos60^\circ=5\text{ ft}.</md></p>
      <p>The height is about <m>8.66</m> feet. Check the lengths with
      <m>5^2+(5\sqrt{3})^2=100=10^2</m>. When using a calculator,
      select degree mode for <m>60^\circ</m>, or enter <m>\pi/3</m> in
      radian mode.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <p>The two acute angles add to <m>90^\circ</m>. Switching to the
    other acute angle swaps opposite and adjacent, so
    <m>\sin\theta=\cos(90^\circ-\theta)</m>. In radians this is
    <m>\sin\theta=\cos(\pi/2-\theta)</m>.</p>
    <p>Right-triangle side lengths are positive. For an angle in another
    quadrant, use its acute reference angle to find the coordinate
    magnitudes, then use the quadrant for signs. For instance,
    <m>150^\circ</m> has reference angle <m>30^\circ</m>, so its
    unit-circle point is <m>(-\sqrt{3}/2,1/2)</m>. The horizontal coordinate
    is negative; the triangle's horizontal side length is positive.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-triangle-check">
      <title>Connect lengths, ratios, and coordinates</title>
      <statement><p><ol>
        <li><p>Relative to <m>\theta</m>, a right triangle has opposite
        side <m>5</m> and adjacent side <m>12</m>. Find the hypotenuse,
        <m>\sin\theta</m>, <m>\cos\theta</m>, and the corresponding
        unit-circle point. Explain why tripling all sides changes neither ratio.</p></li>
        <li><p>A ramp rises <m>2</m> meters and makes an angle of
        <m>30^\circ</m> with the ground. Find its sloping length and
        horizontal run.</p></li>
        <li><p>In the first triangle, let <m>\phi</m> be the other acute
        angle. Find <m>\sin\phi</m> and <m>\cos\phi</m>.</p></li>
      </ol></p></statement>
      <solution><p><ol>
        <li><p>The hypotenuse is <m>\sqrt{5^2+12^2}=13</m>, so
        <m>\sin\theta=5/13</m>, <m>\cos\theta=12/13</m>, and the point
        is <m>(12/13,5/13)</m>. Tripling gives ratios <m>15/39</m> and
        <m>36/39</m>; the common factor cancels.</p></li>
        <li><p>Since <m>\sin30^\circ=2/r=1/2</m>, the ramp length is
        <m>r=4</m> meters. The horizontal run is
        <m>4\cos30^\circ=2\sqrt{3}</m> meters.</p></li>
        <li><p>The legs swap roles: <m>\sin\phi=12/13</m> and
        <m>\cos\phi=5/13</m>.</p></li>
      </ol></p></solution>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="reed-trig-identity">
    <title>One circle, one identity</title>
    <p>Every point <m>(u,v)</m> on the unit circle is one unit from the
    origin. The Pythagorean theorem says <m>u^2+v^2=1</m>. Substitute
    <m>u=\cos\theta</m> and <m>v=\sin\theta</m>:</p>
    <p><md>\boxed{\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta=1.}</md></p>
    <p>That is the whole derivation. The notation <m>\sin^2\theta</m>
    means <m>(\sin\theta)^2</m>, not <m>\sin(\theta^2)</m>. In other
    quadrants, the triangle's side lengths are the absolute values of the
    coordinates. Squaring removes the signs, so the same identity holds for
    every angle, including the axis points.</p>
    <example>
      <title>Recover a missing coordinate</title>
      <statement><p>If <m>\cos\theta=-3/5</m> and <m>\theta</m> is in
      quadrant II, find <m>\sin\theta</m>.</p></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The identity gives</p>
        <p><md>\sin^2\theta=1-\frac{9}{25}=\frac{16}{25}.</md></p>
        <p>So <m>\sin\theta=\pm4/5</m>. Quadrant II has positive vertical
        coordinate, hence <m>\sin\theta=4/5</m>. The identity determines
        the magnitude; the quadrant determines the sign.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-identity-check">
      <title>Explain the identity</title>
      <statement><p>Draw a unit circle and explain the identity in one sentence.
      Then find <m>\cos\theta</m> when <m>\sin\theta=-5/13</m> and
      <m>\theta</m> is in quadrant III. Finally, simplify
      <m>1-\sin^2\theta</m>.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The squared coordinates of a point on a circle of radius
      <m>1</m> sum to <m>1</m>. Here
      <m>\cos^2\theta=1-25/169=144/169</m>; quadrant III makes cosine
      negative, so <m>\cos\theta=-12/13</m>. For every angle,
      <m>1-\sin^2\theta=\cos^2\theta</m>.</p></solution>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="reed-trig-waves">
    <title>From a circle to a wave</title>
    <p>Now let the angle vary and graph its horizontal coordinate against the
    angle. This produces <m>y=\cos x</m>. As the point goes around the circle,
    its horizontal coordinate follows the pattern
    <m>1,0,-1,0,1</m> at the five landmarks above. Plotting the vertical
    coordinate instead gives <m>y=\sin x</m>, with pattern
    <m>0,1,0,-1,0</m>.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Cosine and sine record two coordinates of the same circular
      motion. Both repeat after one full turn.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/sine-cosine.svg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Graphs of cosine and sine from zero to two pi with values
        marked at every quarter turn. Cosine starts at one; sine starts at zero
        and rises. Both return to their initial values after two pi.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>The <term>midline</term> is the line halfway between the maximum and
    minimum; here it is <m>y=0</m>. The <term>amplitude</term> is the distance
    from the midline to a peak; here it is <m>1</m>. The <term>period</term>
    is the length of the shortest positive horizontal shift that repeats the
    entire graph; here it is <m>2\pi</m>. A maximum and the next minimum are
    only half a period apart.</p>
    <p>Sine has the same shape as cosine, shifted right by a quarter turn:</p>
    <p><md>\sin x=\cos\left(x-\frac{\pi}{2}\right).</md></p>
    <p>To see this on the circle, rotate <m>(u,v)</m> clockwise by a quarter
    turn. Its coordinates become <m>(v,-u)</m>, so the new horizontal
    coordinate is the old vertical coordinate. This also lets us describe
    sine waves using the cosine family.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-circle-rotations">
      <title>Practice turns and reflections on the circle</title>
      <!-- Reused from sine_cosine.ptx. -->
      <webwork source="Library/LoyolaChicago/Precalc/Chap6Sec3/Q40.pg"/>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="reed-trig-parameters">
    <title>What A, w, T, and C do</title>
    <p>In <m>f(x)=A\cos(wx+T)+C</m>, call the angle inside cosine
    <m>\theta=wx+T</m>. Starting with an input <m>x</m>, first compute this
    angle, then read its horizontal coordinate on the unit circle, then
    multiply that coordinate by <m>A</m>, and finally add <m>C</m> to the
    result. The parameter <m>w</m> is often
    written as the Greek letter <m>\omega</m>. We use <m>P</m> for the
    period: <m>T</m> here is an angle, not the period.</p>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>A controls vertical size and reflection</title>
      <p>Multiplying cosine by <m>A</m> multiplies its output by <m>A</m>.
      First take <m>C=0</m>. Then
      <m>3\cos x</m> ranges from <m>-3</m> to <m>3</m>, and
      <m>\tfrac12\cos x</m> ranges from <m>-1/2</m> to <m>1/2</m>.
      A negative <m>A</m> also reflects the graph across the horizontal axis:
      <m>-3\cos x</m> begins at <m>-3</m> rather than <m>3</m>.
      Amplitude is a distance, so it is <m>|A|</m>, never negative.
      With any <m>C</m>, the displacement from the midline is
      <m>f(x)-C=A\cos(wx+T)</m>. Changing the sign of <m>A</m> reflects
      the wave across its midline <m>y=C</m>.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-amplitude-read">
      <title>Read amplitude from a graph</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/GraphingSineCosine/identify-amplitude.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-amplitude-match">
      <title>Match amplitudes and reflections</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/Precalculus/setTrigonometry_-_Graphing_Amplitude/matching-amplitudes.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>w controls how quickly the angle changes</title>
      <p>Increasing <m>x</m> by <m>\Delta x</m> changes the angle by
      <m>w\Delta x</m>. A complete turn requires an angular change of size
      <m>2\pi</m>. For <m>A\ne0</m> and <m>w\ne0</m>,</p>
      <p><md>|w|P=2\pi,\qquad \boxed{P=\frac{2\pi}{|w|}.}</md></p>
      <p>For example, <m>\cos(2x)</m> makes a full turn while <m>x</m>
      increases by only <m>\pi</m>; its graph is horizontally compressed.
      The graph of <m>\cos(x/2)</m> needs <m>4\pi</m> units for a turn and
      is horizontally stretched. Larger <m>|w|</m> means a shorter period.</p>
      <p>If <m>w&lt;0</m>, the angle decreases as <m>x</m> increases, so the
      point travels clockwise. The period still uses <m>|w|</m>. Reflecting
      the circle across the horizontal axis shows
      <m>\cos(-\theta)=\cos\theta</m>, so
      <m>\cos(-2x)=\cos(2x)</m>. With a nonzero phase angle, keep track of
      that sign too: <m>\cos(-2x+T)=\cos(2x-T)</m>.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-period-match">
      <title>Match graphs by their periods</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/Precalculus/setTrigonometry_-_Graphing_Period/matching-period.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>T controls the starting angle</title>
      <p>At <m>x=0</m>, the angle is <m>T</m>, so <m>f(0)=A\cos T+C</m>.
      To locate the horizontal shift, factor out <m>w</m>:</p>
      <p><md>w x+T=w\left(x+\frac{T}{w}\right)=w(x-h),
      \qquad \boxed{h=-\frac{T}{w}.}</md></p>
      <p>The graph of <m>A\cos(w(x-h))+C</m> is the graph of
      <m>A\cos(wx)+C</m> shifted right by <m>h</m> if <m>h&gt;0</m>, or left
      by <m>|h|</m> if <m>h&lt;0</m>. Equivalently, solve <m>w x+T=0</m>
      to find where the original angle-zero point has moved.</p>
      <p>For example, <m>\cos(2x+\pi/2)=\cos(2(x+\pi/4))</m>
      shifts <m>\cos(2x)</m> left by <m>\pi/4</m>, not <m>\pi/2</m>.
      The plus sign describes a head start around the circle. The graph reaches
      a given angle earlier, which explains the shift to the left when
      <m>w&gt;0</m>.</p>
      <p>Changing <m>T</m> does not change amplitude or period. Adding
      <m>2\pi k</m> to <m>T</m>, for any integer <m>k</m>, gives the same
      function; horizontal shifts that differ by whole periods are equivalent.
      If you change <m>w</m> while keeping <m>T</m> fixed, both the period
      and the shift <m>-T/w</m> generally change.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs xml:id="reed-trig-vertical-displacement">
      <title>C controls vertical displacement</title>
      <p>The <m>+C</m> sits outside cosine, so it adds the same amount to
      every output. If <m>C&gt;0</m>, the graph moves up by <m>C</m>; if
      <m>C&lt;0</m>, it moves down by <m>|C|</m>. The midline becomes
      <m>y=C</m>. Taking <m>C=0</m> gives a wave centered on the horizontal
      axis.</p>
      <p>For a nonconstant wave,</p>
      <p><md>\boxed{\text{maximum}=C+|A|,\qquad
      \text{minimum}=C-|A|.}</md></p>
      <p>For example, <m>2\cos(3x)+4</m> has midline <m>y=4</m> and
      range <m>[2,6]</m>. Its amplitude is still <m>2</m>, and its period
      is still <m>2\pi/3</m>. The graph of <m>\cos x-4</m> has midline
      <m>y=-4</m> and range <m>[-5,-3]</m>.</p>
      <p>Changing <m>C</m> leaves amplitude, period, and horizontal shift
      unchanged. Keep the two additions separate: <m>T</m> is added to
      the angle <em>inside</em> cosine and changes the horizontal shift;
      <m>C</m> is added to the output <em>outside</em> cosine and changes
      the vertical position. In particular, the maximum height is not
      necessarily the amplitude.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <table>
      <title>The nonconstant family at a glance</title>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes"><cell>Feature</cell><cell><m>A\cos(wx+T)+C</m>, with <m>A,w\ne0</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Amplitude</cell><cell><m>|A|</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Midline and range</cell><cell><m>y=C</m>; <m>[C-|A|,C+|A|]</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Period</cell><cell><m>P=2\pi/|w|</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Horizontal shift</cell><cell><m>h=-T/w</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Value at zero</cell><cell><m>A\cos T+C</m></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>Two exceptions deserve their own check: if <m>A=0</m>, then
    <m>f(x)=C</m>; if <m>w=0</m>, then <m>f(x)=A\cos T+C</m> is constant.
    Constant functions have no smallest positive period. In particular, do not
    divide by <m>w</m> when <m>w=0</m>.</p>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-shift-check">
      <title>Read the inside and outside carefully</title>
      <statement><p>For <m>g(x)=-2\cos(3x-\pi)+5</m>, find the amplitude,
      period, horizontal shift, midline, range, and <m>g(0)</m>. Explain why
      its amplitude is not negative, its horizontal shift is not <m>\pi</m>,
      and the <m>+5</m> does not change the period.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The amplitude is <m>2</m>, the period is <m>2\pi/3</m>,
      and <m>3x-\pi=3(x-\pi/3)</m> gives a shift right by
      <m>\pi/3</m>. The midline is <m>y=5</m>, the range is
      <m>[3,7]</m>, and <m>g(0)=-2\cos(-\pi)+5=7</m>. The minus sign
      reflects the graph across its midline; amplitude is a positive distance.
      The phase angle must be divided by the coefficient of <m>x</m> to
      find the horizontal shift. Adding <m>5</m> changes heights, not the
      rate at which the angle changes.</p></solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-phase-graphs">
      <title>Write equations for shifted cosine graphs</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/Precalculus/setTrigonometry_-_Graphing_Phase_Shift/short-answer-phaseshift-cosine.pg"/>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="reed-trig-put-together">
    <title>Build a graph, then read an equation from a graph</title>
    <p>For <m>w&gt;0</m>, the five quarter-turn landmarks occur at
    <m>x=h,h+P/4,h+P/2,h+3P/4,h+P</m>. Their heights are
    <m>C+A,C,C-A,C,C+A</m>. This works even when <m>A&lt;0</m>: the first point
    is then a minimum. Join the landmarks with a smooth wave and repeat.</p>
    <example xml:id="reed-trig-worked-wave">
      <title>Sketch <m>f(x)=3\cos(2x+\pi/2)+2</m></title>
      <statement><p>Find its amplitude, period, horizontal shift, midline,
      and range, and sketch one cycle.</p></statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Here <m>A=3</m>, <m>w=2</m>, <m>T=\pi/2</m>, and <m>C=2</m>. The
        amplitude is <m>3</m>, <m>P=\pi</m>, and <m>h=-\pi/4</m>.
        Each quarter period has length <m>\pi/4</m>. The midline is
        <m>y=2</m> and the range is <m>[-1,5]</m>. Add <m>2</m> to each
        height of the corresponding wave with <m>C=0</m>.</p>
        <table>
          <title>Follow the angle inside cosine</title>
          <tabular halign="center">
            <row header="yes"><cell><m>x</m></cell><cell><m>-\pi/4</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\pi/4</m></cell><cell><m>\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>3\pi/4</m></cell></row>
            <row><cell><m>2x+\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>\pi</m></cell><cell><m>3\pi/2</m></cell><cell><m>2\pi</m></cell></row>
            <row><cell><m>f(x)</m></cell><cell><m>5</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>5</m></cell></row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>The graph starts this cycle at a maximum, falls through the midline,
        reaches a minimum, and returns to the maximum. As a check,
        <m>f(0)=3\cos(\pi/2)+2=2</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <figure>
      <caption>One cycle of <m>3\cos(2x+\pi/2)+2</m>. The midline is
      <m>y=2</m>, the amplitude is <m>3</m>, and consecutive peaks are
      <m>\pi</m> apart. Adding <m>2</m> moves every point up by <m>2</m>.</caption>
      <image source="reed-algebra-lessons/transformed-cosine.svg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>A cosine wave through the five points negative pi over four,
        five; zero, two; pi over four, negative one; pi over two, two; and
        three pi over four, five. The dashed midline is y equals two.
        Amplitude three, period pi, and vertical displacement two are marked.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <example>
      <title>Choose parameters from a graph</title>
      <statement><p>A cosine-shaped wave has maximum <m>7</m>, minimum
      <m>3</m>, and consecutive maxima at <m>x=1</m> and <m>x=5</m>.
      Find an equation.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The midline is halfway between the extreme heights:
      <m>C=(7+3)/2=5</m>. The amplitude is half their difference:
      <m>|A|=(7-3)/2=2</m>. Choose <m>A=2</m>. The period is
      <m>5-1=4</m>, so choose <m>w=2\pi/4=\pi/2</m>. Place a peak at
      <m>h=1</m>:</p>
      <p><md>f(x)=2\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}(x-1)\right)+5
      =2\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}x-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)+5.</md></p>
      <p>Thus one choice is <m>T=-\pi/2</m>. Check that the minimum lies
      halfway between the two maxima, at <m>x=3</m>, where <m>f(3)=3</m>.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <p>In general, if a nonconstant wave has maximum <m>M</m> and minimum
    <m>m</m>, recover its vertical displacement and amplitude using</p>
    <p><md>\boxed{C=\frac{M+m}{2},\qquad |A|=\frac{M-m}{2}.}</md></p>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-graph-match">
      <title>Combine amplitude and period</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/GraphingSineCosine/function-graphing.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>The same rules for sine</title>
      <p>The same parameter rules apply to <m>A\sin(wx+T)+C</m>:
      amplitude <m>|A|</m>, period <m>2\pi/|w|</m>, horizontal shift
      <m>-T/w</m>, and midline <m>y=C</m>, when <m>A,w\ne0</m>.</p>
      <p>In shifted form, write the wave as <m>A\sin(w(x-h))+C</m>.
      For <m>w&gt;0</m>, its quarter-cycle heights, starting at <m>x=h</m>, are
      <m>C,C+A,C,C-A,C</m>. If you convert it to cosine using the
      quarter-turn identity, the corresponding cosine phase angle is
      <m>-wh-\pi/2</m>. The vertical displacement <m>C</m> stays the same.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-complete-features">
      <title>Describe a shifted sine wave</title>
      <webwork source="Contrib/CCCS/PreCalculus/08.1/CCD_CCCS_Openstax_AlgTrig_AT-1-001-AS_8_1_22.pg"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-exit-check">
      <title>Check your understanding</title>
      <statement>
        <p><ol>
          <li><p>Sketch one cycle of <m>2\cos(x/2-\pi/2)-1</m> by listing
          its five quarter-turn points. State its amplitude, period, horizontal
          shift, midline, and range.</p></li>
          <li><p>A wave with midline <m>-3</m> has amplitude <m>4</m>,
          period <m>6</m>, and a maximum at <m>x=-1</m>. Write one
          equation in the form <m>A\cos(wx+T)+C</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>A classmate says <m>\cos^2(2x)+\sin^2(2x)=2</m>
          because the angle doubles. Explain the mistake using the circle.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><ol>
          <li><p>Amplitude <m>2</m>, period <m>4\pi</m>, shift right
          <m>\pi</m>, midline <m>y=-1</m>, and range <m>[-3,1]</m>.
          The points are <m>(\pi,1)</m>, <m>(2\pi,-1)</m>,
          <m>(3\pi,-3)</m>, <m>(4\pi,-1)</m>, and <m>(5\pi,1)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Choose <m>A=4</m>, <m>w=\pi/3</m>, and
          <m>T=\pi/3</m>, with <m>C=-3</m>, giving
          <m>4\cos(\pi x/3+\pi/3)-3</m>.
          At <m>x=-1</m> the angle is zero; six units later it is
          <m>2\pi</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The angle <m>2x</m> still locates a point on a circle of
          radius <m>1</m>. Its squared coordinates sum to <m>1</m>.
          Changing the angle changes the point, not the radius.</p></li>
        </ol></p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="reed-trig-other-functions">
    <title>Four more functions: tangent, cotangent, secant, and cosecant</title>
    <p>Sine and cosine give the coordinates of a point on the unit circle.
    The other four trigonometric functions are ratios or reciprocals of
    those same coordinates. They do not require a new circle or a new kind
    of angle.</p>
    <table>
      <title>Build the other four functions from sine and cosine</title>
      <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes"><cell>Function</cell><cell>Definition</cell><cell>Right-triangle ratio</cell><cell>Defined when</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Tangent</cell><cell><m>\tan\theta=\sin\theta/\cos\theta</m></cell><cell>opposite / adjacent</cell><cell><m>\cos\theta\ne0</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Cotangent</cell><cell><m>\cot\theta=\cos\theta/\sin\theta</m></cell><cell>adjacent / opposite</cell><cell><m>\sin\theta\ne0</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Secant</cell><cell><m>\sec\theta=1/\cos\theta</m></cell><cell>hypotenuse / adjacent</cell><cell><m>\cos\theta\ne0</m></cell></row>
        <row><cell>Cosecant</cell><cell><m>\csc\theta=1/\sin\theta</m></cell><cell>hypotenuse / opposite</cell><cell><m>\sin\theta\ne0</m></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>The triangle ratios apply to acute angles in a right triangle. For
    example, dividing <m>\sin\theta=b/r</m> by <m>\cos\theta=a/r</m>
    cancels the hypotenuse and gives <m>\tan\theta=b/a</m>. Together with
    sine and cosine, tangent completes the mnemonic <em>SOH–CAH–TOA</em>:
    sine is opposite over hypotenuse, cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse,
    and tangent is opposite over adjacent.</p>
    <p>On the unit circle, write the point as <m>(u,v)</m>. Then tangent
    is <m>v/u</m>: the slope of the line from the origin to the point.
    Cotangent is <m>u/v</m>, secant is <m>1/u</m>, and cosecant is
    <m>1/v</m>. These coordinate formulas work in all quadrants wherever
    their denominators are nonzero.</p>
    <example xml:id="reed-trig-tangent-height">
      <title>Find a height without knowing the hypotenuse</title>
      <statement><p>You stand <m>20</m> meters horizontally from a tower.
      From a point at ground level, the angle up to its top is
      <m>30^\circ</m>. Find the tower's height.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The height <m>h</m> is opposite the angle, and the
      known distance <m>20</m> is adjacent. Use tangent:</p>
      <p><md>\tan30^\circ=\frac{h}{20},\qquad
      h=20\frac{1/2}{\sqrt{3}/2}=\frac{20\sqrt{3}}{3}\text{ m}.</md></p>
      <p>The height is about <m>11.55</m> meters. No hypotenuse length
      was needed.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="reed-trig-six-values">
      <title>One point determines all six values</title>
      <statement><p>The unit-circle point for <m>\theta</m> is
      <m>(-3/5,4/5)</m>. Find all six trigonometric values.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>Read cosine and sine first, then take ratios and
      reciprocals:</p>
      <p><md>\cos\theta=-\frac35,\quad \sin\theta=\frac45,\quad
      \tan\theta=-\frac43,\quad \cot\theta=-\frac34,\quad
      \sec\theta=-\frac53,\quad \csc\theta=\frac54.</md></p>
      <p>In quadrant II, sine and cosecant are positive; cosine, secant,
      tangent, and cotangent are negative.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Undefined values and reciprocals</title>
      <p>Division by zero is undefined. At <m>\theta=\pi/2</m>, the
      point is <m>(0,1)</m>: tangent and secant are undefined, while
      cotangent is <m>0</m> and cosecant is <m>1</m>. At <m>\theta=0</m>,
      tangent is <m>0</m> and secant is <m>1</m>, but cotangent and
      cosecant are undefined.</p>
      <p>In general, tangent and secant are undefined at
      <m>\theta=\pi/2+k\pi</m>; cotangent and cosecant are undefined at
      <m>\theta=k\pi</m>, where <m>k</m> is any integer. An undefined
      value is not a value of infinity.</p>
      <p>Where both are defined, tangent and cotangent are reciprocals.
      But <m>\cot\theta=\cos\theta/\sin\theta</m> also defines cotangent
      at angles such as <m>\pi/2</m>, where tangent itself is undefined.</p>
      <p>Reciprocal is different from inverse function:
      <m>\csc\theta=1/\sin\theta</m>, whereas <m>\arcsin(t)</m>
      asks for an angle whose sine is <m>t</m>. Calculator notation
      <m>\sin^{-1}(t)</m> usually means arcsine, not cosecant.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>How their graphs differ from sine and cosine</title>
      <p>After a half turn, both circle coordinates change sign. Their
      ratio stays the same, so tangent and cotangent have period
      <m>\pi</m>. Secant and cosecant have period <m>2\pi</m>.
      Each function has vertical asymptotes at the angles where its
      denominator is zero, and each is unbounded. Thus none has an
      amplitude like a sine or cosine wave.</p>
      <p>Vertical displacement works here too: adding <m>C</m> to any of
      these functions moves every defined output up or down by <m>C</m>.
      For example, <m>y=\tan x+2</m> passes through <m>(0,2)</m> instead
      of <m>(0,0)</m>. Its period and the locations of its vertical
      asymptotes are unchanged.</p>
      <p>There are two more identities hiding in the original one.
      Dividing <m>\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1</m> by
      <m>\cos^2\theta</m>, when cosine is nonzero, gives
      <m>\tan^2\theta+1=\sec^2\theta</m>. Dividing by
      <m>\sin^2\theta</m>, when sine is nonzero, gives
      <m>1+\cot^2\theta=\csc^2\theta</m>.</p>
    </paragraphs>
    <exercise xml:id="reed-trig-other-functions-check">
      <title>Use ratios, signs, and domains</title>
      <statement><p><ol>
        <li><p>A right triangle has opposite side <m>8</m>, adjacent
        side <m>15</m>, and hypotenuse <m>17</m>, relative to
        <m>\theta</m>. Find all six trigonometric values.</p></li>
        <li><p>Find <m>\tan(5\pi/6)</m>, <m>\cot(5\pi/6)</m>,
        <m>\sec(5\pi/6)</m>, and <m>\csc(5\pi/6)</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>At <m>\theta=\pi</m>, which of the six functions are
        undefined? Give the values of the other four.</p></li>
        <li><p>Someone says <m>\sec\theta</m> must lie between
        <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m> because cosine does. Give a counterexample
        and explain why taking a reciprocal changes the bound.</p></li>
      </ol></p></statement>
      <solution><p><ol>
        <li><p><m>\sin\theta=8/17</m>, <m>\cos\theta=15/17</m>,
        <m>\tan\theta=8/15</m>, <m>\cot\theta=15/8</m>,
        <m>\sec\theta=17/15</m>, <m>\csc\theta=17/8</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Since cosine is <m>-\sqrt{3}/2</m> and sine is
        <m>1/2</m>, the values are respectively <m>-\sqrt{3}/3</m>,
        <m>-\sqrt{3}</m>, <m>-2\sqrt{3}/3</m>, and <m>2</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>The point is <m>(-1,0)</m>. Cotangent and cosecant are
        undefined. The others are <m>\sin\pi=0</m>, <m>\cos\pi=-1</m>,
        <m>\tan\pi=0</m>, and <m>\sec\pi=-1</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><m>\sec(\pi/3)=1/(1/2)=2</m>. For nonzero cosine,
        <m>0&lt;|\cos\theta|\le1</m>, so
        <m>|\sec\theta|\ge1</m>. A nonzero denominator close to zero
        makes the reciprocal's magnitude very large.</p></li>
      </ol></p></solution>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
